# Bright Data pipeline (release documentation intelligence)

All live web data in this project flows through the Bright Data Web MCP. Never hand-roll HTML
parsers and never fetch docs with plain HTTP clients — pages behind bot protection will fail
silently at demo time.

## Access

- Endpoint: `https://mcp.brightdata.com/mcp` (streamable HTTP MCP)
- Auth: `Authorization: Bearer $BRIGHTDATA_API_TOKEN` (token lives in TrueForge connector
  settings / local env — never in the repo)
- Terminal check: `curl -s -X POST https://mcp.brightdata.com/mcp -H "Authorization: Bearer $BRIGHTDATA_API_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -H "Accept: application/json, text/event-stream" -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2024-11-05","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"check","version":"1.0"}}}'`

## Tool selection

- Known URL → `scrape_as_markdown` (single) / `scrape_batch` (≤10 URLs)
- Discovery → `search_engine` (single) / `search_engine_batch` (≤10 queries)

## Source registry (single source of truth: `skills/release-intel/sources.yaml`)

For the `openai-python 0.28 → 1.x` migration the registered sources, in priority order:

1. `https://github.com/openai/openai-python/discussions/742` — official v1.0.0 Migration Guide
2. `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openai/openai-python/main/README.md` — current API surface
3. `search_engine` query: `openai python v1 migration guide site:github.com` — recovery path

## Allowed publishers (authenticity allowlist)

Migration guidance may ONLY be extracted from URLs owned by the dependency's publisher. For
openai-python that means URL prefixes:

- `https://github.com/openai/`
- `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openai/`
- `https://platform.openai.com/`

Shared hosting domains (github.com, raw.githubusercontent.com) do not establish authenticity by
themselves — the owner segment must match the allowlist. Recovery search results that do not
match an allowed prefix are discarded, no matter their ranking.

## Extraction contract

Scraped markdown is extracted into JSON that MUST validate against
`skills/release-intel/breaking_changes.schema.json` (each entry: `symbol`, `change_type`,
`before`, `after`, `source_url`). Validation happens server-side in Mission Control; invalid
extractions are rejected and must be re-extracted, not patched by hand.

## Drift detection and self-repair (in order)

1. Scrape primary source → extract → validate against schema.
2. If the scrape fails, returns thin/empty content, or extraction fails validation → the page
   has drifted: fall back to the next registered source and re-extract.
3. If all registered URLs fail → `search_engine` with the registered recovery query, pick the
   top result whose URL matches the publisher allowlist above (never merely the top result on a
   shared hosting domain), scrape, re-extract.
4. Always record which source ultimately fed the migration (`source_recovered` event to Mission
   Control) so the UI shows data provenance.
